  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 224 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure centered over the Southeast will continue \nto drive clear skies and cold northwest air into the region through \ntonight. With clear skies remaining into tonight, strong radiational \ncooling will take place with many locations seeing the coldest air \nof the season, with near record cold across portions of Vermont. \nTemperatures will fall to the low 20s for most of the region, \noutside of the higher terrain where a steep inversion will keep lows \nin the upper 20s to near 30. Near record cold at our climate sites \nincluding Montpelier, St. Johnsbury, and Plattsburgh will be \npossible tonight. A few high clouds late tonight look to limit the \nprime radiational cooling time tonight across much of northern New \nYork, otherwise a few records there might also be in jeopardy.\n\nTemperatures will gradually warm and trend to normal as high \npressure shifts east offshore Wednesday turning winds to the south, \nadvecting in warmer air. A glancing mid level trough will bring some \ncloudy skies through the area tomorrow, but surface-mid levels will \nbe too dry to see any precipitation. Temperatures will be 5-10 \ndegrees warmer tomorrow with highs in the low to mid 50s, with our \ncool spots near the International Border and the Northeast Kingdom.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: By Thursday, southwest strong waa ushered in by a 45-55 \nknot 850 LLJ will warm temperatures steeply well into the 60s. Highs \nin the mid to upper 60s will be possible with areas in the St. \nLawrence Valley nearing even 70. The warm temperatures will come at \na price though, as southwest winds will increase to 20-30 MPH, \nparticularly in the St. Lawrence and Champlain Valleys. Model \nsoundings continue to trend up with top of the mixed layer in the \nrecent model suite averaging around 35 MPH. Mixing of these stronger \nwinds will be complex as the timing of the precipitation associated \nwith the cold front will be entering the region around the time of \npeak mixing, which could temper gusts slightly lower. Regardless, \nThursday afternoon in the St. Lawrence Valley and near Lake \nChamplain will be gusty. Precipitation will generally be scattered \nwith the front due to lingering dry air at the surface, though \nmoisture advection with this system will be better than our system \nMonday. A few hundreths up to a tenth of an inch or rainfall is \nexpected, focused in the St. Lawrence Valley, Adirondacks, and \nnorthern Greens. A few showers will linger into Friday along the \nnorthern mountains, but we will begin to trend drier by Friday \nafternoon. Winds will turn to the west northwest behind the front \nwhich will bring drier and cooler air by Friday afternoon. Lapse \nrates will steepen Friday afternoon as we dry out with good vertical \nmixing across our northern wider valleys. Winds will increase with \nbetter chances for seeing some stronger winds up to 30 MPH. Have \ntrended winds up as confidence grows for these stronger channeled \nwinds Friday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Surface high pressure builds over our region for the \nweekend bringing dry weather and temperatures slightly warmer than \nseasonal normals for daytime highs, and cooler than normal overnight \nminimum temperatures. Sunday night into early next week high \npressure moves east of our region and will have some shower chances \nnext week, though lots of uncertainty at this stage.",
